# API and Data Fetching Rules

## Server Actions and API Routes
- Use Server Actions as the primary method for mutations
- Implement proper error handling with Server Actions
- Use Next.js Route Handlers for complex API endpoints
- Leverage Next.js Edge Runtime for performance-critical routes
- Use proper TypeScript types for action parameters and responses
- Implement proper Zod validation for all inputs

## Data Fetching Patterns
- Use React Server Components (RSC) as the default approach
- Implement @tanstack/react-query for client-side state management
- Use proper suspense boundaries with React Suspense
- Leverage Next.js cache() and revalidate patterns
- Implement proper error boundaries with react-error-boundary
- Use proper loading states with React Suspense

## React Query Best Practices
- Use proper query keys with type safety
- Implement proper mutation handlers
- Use proper query invalidation
- Leverage optimistic updates
- Implement proper retry logic
- Use proper prefetching strategies
- Handle infinite queries properly

## State Management
- Use Server Components state by default
- Implement URL state with nuqs:
  - Use searchParamsState for simple URL state
  - Implement parseAsString for text parameters
  - Use parseAsFloat/parseAsInteger for numbers
  - Implement parseAsArrayOf for arrays
  - Use parseAsBoolean for boolean flags
  - Implement parseAsJson for complex objects
  - Use createSearchParamsCache for performance
  - Handle URL state serialization properly
- Use React Query for server state
- Use Zustand for client-side state management:
  - Create atomic stores for specific features
  - Use TypeScript for type-safe stores
  - Implement proper devtools integration
  - Use proper middleware (persist, devtools)
  - Keep stores small and focused
  - Use proper selectors for performance
  - Implement proper store composition
- Keep client state minimal
- Use form state with react-hook-form
- Implement proper Zod validation
- Handle hydration properly
- Use URL state for shareable data

